    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Top Picks</a>
          <a href="#cfm-vs-mph" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. CFM vs MPH</a>
          <a href="#yard-size" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Sizing to Your Yard</a>
          <a href="#runtime" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Batteries and Runtime</a>
          <a href="#noise" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Noise and Local Rules</a>
          <a href="#technique" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Blowing Technique</a>
          <a href="#after-the-blow" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Handling the Leaves</a>
          <a href="#platforms" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Choosing a Platform</a>
          <a href="#gas-comparison" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Gas or Corded?</a>
          <a href="#care-and-safety" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Care and Safety</a>
          <a href="#cost" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. Cost and Value</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">12. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">13. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A cordless blower is the battery tool that most often disappoints new owners, and the reason is almost always the same: they bought on the speed number printed on the box, ran it on turbo, and watched the battery die in ten minutes with half the lawn still covered. Buy on airflow, carry a second battery for big jobs, and save turbo for the wet stuff.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />


        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The 7 Best Cordless Leaf Blowers, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            These picks are based on manufacturer-listed airflow in CFM and air speed in MPH, battery platform and capacity, published noise ratings where available, weight, warranty, and recurring patterns in owner feedback. We favored blowers that pair strong airflow with a battery system you can grow into. No rankings come from measurements we did not make.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} index={index} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="cfm-vs-mph" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. CFM vs MPH: The Number That Moves Leaves
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every blower box lists two numbers. <strong>CFM</strong>, cubic feet per minute, is the volume of air leaving the nozzle. <strong>MPH</strong> is the speed of that air. Manufacturers often lead with MPH because big speed numbers look impressive, but volume is what actually moves a layer of leaves across a lawn. A blower with high speed and low volume makes a narrow, fast jet that pushes a stripe of leaves; a blower with high volume moves a wide swath of debris in each pass.
          </p>

          <p>
            Speed still matters for one job: lifting wet, matted leaves and debris that is stuck to pavement or wedged into turf. A fast stream gets under the edge of a leaf and peels it up. Once leaves are loose, volume does the heavy lifting. The best blowers combine high values of both, and some manufacturers also publish a combined force rating in newtons, which is a useful single comparison number where it is available.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Rough Airflow Guide</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">300-450</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">CFM for decks, patios and walks</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">450-600</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">CFM for most suburban yards</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">600+</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">CFM for big lots and wet leaves</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Turbo</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Short bursts only to save battery</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Be careful comparing ratings across brands, since there is no single mandatory test that every manufacturer follows for consumer blowers, and some list the highest figure from a turbo setting while others list sustained output. Treat the numbers as broad classes rather than exact rankings, and compare figures that are measured the same way, such as both turbo or both maximum non-turbo, when a manufacturer publishes both.
          </p>

          <p>
            Weight is the hidden third number. A high-output blower with a large battery can weigh much more at the handle than a compact one, and you feel every pound in a 40-minute session. If your yard is small, a lighter blower with modest airflow is the more pleasant tool.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="yard-size" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Matching the Blower to Your Yard
          </h2>

          <p>
            Small properties, roughly a tenth of an acre or less, with a deck, patio and short driveway are well served by a compact blower in the 300 to 450 CFM range. The tool is light, quick to grab, and a single battery covers the whole job. The Worx LEAFJET and Makita XBU02 fit here, as do the smaller blowers on most battery platforms.
          </p>

          <p>
            Typical suburban lots, a quarter to half acre with a few trees, are where full-size handheld battery blowers in the 500 to 650-plus CFM range shine. The EGO LB6504 and Ryobi 40V HP Whisper Series are the two strongest options for most people here. Plan on one large battery for routine cleanups and a second for the heaviest fall weekends, when a mature oak or maple can drop more leaves than any one battery can move.
          </p>

