        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying on MPH Alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Air speed is the most advertised number, but airflow volume in CFM moves most leaves. A high-speed, low-volume blower makes a narrow jet that pushes a stripe of debris and wastes your time on a big lawn. Compare CFM first, use MPH as a secondary number for wet leaves, and look at combined force where it is published.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running Turbo All the Time</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Turbo drains batteries several times faster than moderate settings, and most leaf piles move fine at lower speed. New owners who hold turbo the whole session are the ones who complain about runtime. Save it for wet patches and stuck debris, and use cruise control for steady passes.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a Platform You Will Not Grow Into</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A blower bought on sale from a brand that offers no mower, trimmer or chainsaw you would want leaves you with orphaned batteries. Before buying, look at the platform's outdoor lineup and pick the one that covers the tools you will need over the next few years.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Blowing Leaves Into the Street</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Leaves blown into gutters clog storm drains, cause localized flooding, and add nutrients to streams as they rot. Many towns ban it. Blow leaves onto the lawn for mulching, into beds as mulch, or onto a tarp for compost or proper curbside collection. Check local collection rules first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping Eye and Ear Protection</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Blowers throw grit and small stones hard enough to injure eyes, and high-output models on turbo are loud at the operator's ear. Impact-rated safety glasses are a must, and hearing protection is a good idea for longer sessions. Keep the nozzle pointed away from people and pets.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

